      "title": "從照顧到參與：社會參與作為健康支持策略",
      "title_en": "From Care to Participation: Social Participation as a Health Support Strategy",
      "abstract": "面對全球高齡化及失智人口持續增加，健康支持的內涵已由以疾病治療與照顧服務為核心，逐漸轉向重視健康老化、自主生活、社會參與及社區共融。世界衛生組織的健康老化理念強調，健康係由個體能力、支持性環境及功能能力共同形塑，而社會參與是維持健康與福祉的重要基礎。本文以「從照顧到參與」為核心命題，探討社會參與如何作為健康支持策略。研究採文獻分析、參與式觀察及反思分析，並以結構化、聚焦式案例比較整理資料；比較範圍與原投稿一致，包括英國社會處方、日本認知症咖啡館、荷蘭Hogeweyk，以及臺灣Young Coffee、文化健康站與失智友善社區。研究者參訪Young Coffee、文化健康站及長期照顧實務經驗，僅作為理解情境、辨識參與歷程與提出反思的材料，不等同介入成效證據。\n\n分析以支持性環境、社會角色、社區互動與家庭支持為基礎，進一步整理為五項彼此連結的構面：能力與個別差異辨識、支持性環境建構、社會角色維持、社區互動與家庭支持，以及制度接軌。比較顯示，不同案例雖受文化背景、政策制度、服務對象與空間尺度影響，仍共同透過環境調整、職務或任務再設計、文化參與、關係支持與社區連結，降低高齡者及失智者的參與障礙，使其由照顧接受者轉向服務提供者、文化傳承者或社區成員。健康支持的核心因此不只在增加照顧供給，更在創造持續參與社會的機會；社會參與不只是健康促進的結果，也是可能支持功能能力、自我效能、生命尊嚴與生活品質的重要策略。然而，現有案例多屬經驗、敘事或可行性資料，不宜直接推論認知改善、延緩失能、減少醫療利用或降低照顧成本。後續研究應透過共同設計、質性歷程研究、分層結果測量、長期追蹤及現實主義比較評估，回答何種參與機制對何人、在何種情境下發揮作用。研究成果可作為健康老化、社區照顧、失智友善社區及長期照顧政策發展之參考。",
      "abstract_en": "As global population ageing and the prevalence of dementia continue to increase, health support has gradually shifted from a disease- and care-oriented approach toward healthy ageing, autonomous living, social participation, and community inclusion. Under the central proposition of ‘from care to participation,’ this article argues that social participation is not merely an outcome of health promotion but an important strategy of health support. The study employs literature review, participant observation, and reflective analysis, with a structured, focused comparison used to organize evidence from Social Prescribing in the United Kingdom, Dementia Cafés in Japan, Hogeweyk in the Netherlands, and Young Coffee, Cultural Health Stations, and dementia-friendly communities in Taiwan. Field visits and long-term care practice experience are treated as reflective material for interpreting contexts and participation processes rather than as evidence of intervention effects. Focusing on supportive environments, social roles, community interaction, and family support, the analysis develops five connected dimensions: recognition of capabilities and individual differences, construction of supportive environments, maintenance of social roles, community interaction and family support, and institutional linkage. Across different cultural and policy contexts, the cases reduce participation barriers through environmental adaptation, job or task redesign, cultural engagement, relational support, and community connection, enabling older adults and people living with dementia to move from care recipients toward service providers, cultural knowledge holders, or community members. The essence of health support therefore lies not only in providing more care but also in creating opportunities for continued participation. Existing evidence, however, is mainly experiential, narrative, or feasibility-based and cannot establish causal effects on cognition, disability, health-care utilization, or care costs. Future research should combine co-design, qualitative process inquiry, multilevel outcome measurement, longitudinal follow-up, and realist comparative evaluation to determine what mechanisms work for whom and under what circumstances.",
      "keywords": "社會參與、健康支持、健康老化、社會處方、失智友善社區",
      "keywords_en": "Social Participation; Health Support; Healthy Ageing; Social Prescribing; Dementia-Friendly Community",
      "display_presenters": "范家榮",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "范家榮 兼任助理教授｜國立臺北大學社會工作學系"
      ],
