      "title": "成人教育對高齡家庭照顧者心理韌性提升之研究： 以照顧負荷緩解為視角",
      "title_en": "A Study of Adult Education for Enhancing Psychological Resilience among Older Family Caregivers: A Caregiving-Burden Alleviation Perspective",
      "abstract": "研究背景與目的 隨著臺灣邁入超高齡社會，少子化與家庭照顧人力不足使高齡家庭照顧者面臨沉重照顧負荷與身心壓力。然而，現行支持服務多著重照顧技巧訓練，較少從成人教育與終身學習觀點探討其學習需求及心理支持。本研究旨在建構家庭照顧支持服務創新課程模組，探討成人教育介入對高齡家庭照顧者照顧負荷、健康識能及心理韌性之影響。\n\n研究方法或資料來源 研究採以質性為主、量化為輔的混合方法設計，以立意取樣招募完整參與課程之7位65至74歲高齡家庭照顧者（男性2位、女性5位）；7位皆完成半結構式訪談及前、後測。介入內容包括12堂照顧知能課程，涵蓋中醫健康照護、健康識能衛教及照顧技巧各4堂，以及6次封閉式情緒支持團體。質性資料經錄音謄寫、編碼與類屬分析，以歸納照顧經驗與學習需求；量化部分以22題照顧者負荷量表及6題中文版簡版復原力量表（Brief Resilience Scale, BRS）進行前、後測平均數比較，並與訪談結果進行整合詮釋。\n\n研究發現\n\n結果顯示，受訪者的照顧壓力主要來自生理健康退化、心理負擔、經濟壓力、社交限縮及長照資源資訊不足。前、後測描述性結果顯示，5位參與者的照顧負荷下降，整體平均由31.29降至29.14；5位參與者的BRS分數提升，整體平均由2.98升至3.17。訪談亦呈現課程與支持性團體有助於參與者理解照顧技巧與資源運用，並透過經驗分享與情緒支持增加社會連結；惟部分個案因照顧情境變動或既有支持條件不同，無明顯差異。\n\n結論\n\n綜合質、量資料，本方案顯示為高齡家庭照顧者提供實務學習與心理支持的初步可行性；惟受限於樣本數少、未設對照組且量化分析僅為描述性比較，尚不能推論介入效果。未來宜擴大樣本、納入健康識能與照顧自我效能等對應測量，並以推論統計與長期追蹤進一步驗證成效。研究成果可作為長照政策及家庭照顧支持服務設計之參考，回應長壽社會下高齡照顧者多元需求，並提供高齡福祉與服務創新之實證基礎。",
      "abstract_en": "A Study of Adult Education for Enhancing Psychological Resilience among Older Family Caregivers: A Caregiving-Burden Alleviation Perspective Chia-Tzu Lin Department of Adult and Continuing Education, National Chung Cheng University / Doctoral Student\n\nPreparatory Office of National Museum of Modern Art Tainan /Section Chief, Education and Outreach Department Background and Objectives Taiwan’s entry into a super-aged society, together with low fertility and limited family caregiving capacity, places older family caregivers under substantial burden and distress. Existing services emphasize caregiving skills, with less attention to learning needs and psychological support from adult-education and lifelong-learning perspectives. This study developed an innovative curriculum module for family caregiver support services and explored its association with caregiver burden and psychological resilience while documenting health-literacy-related learning.\n\nMethods\n\nA qualitative-dominant mixed-methods design was used. Purposive sampling recruited seven family caregivers aged 65–74 years who completed the program (two men and five women); all completed semi-structured interviews and pre- and post-intervention assessments. The program comprised 12 sessions—four each on traditional Chinese medicine–based health care, health literacy education, and caregiving skills—and six closed-format emotional support group sessions. Interviews were transcribed, coded, and analyzed categorically. Pre- and post-intervention mean scores on a 22-item Caregiver Burden Scale and the six-item Chinese Brief Resilience Scale (BRS) were descriptively compared and integrated with interview findings.\n\nFindings\n\nPrincipal sources of stress were deteriorating physical health, psychological strain, financial stress, social restriction, and inadequate information on long-term care resources. Caregiving-burden scores decreased for five participants, with the mean declining from 31.29 to 29.14; BRS scores increased for five, with the mean rising from 2.98 to 3.17. Interviews suggested that the program enhanced understanding of caregiving skills and resource use, while experience sharing and emotional support fostered social connections. Changes were not uniform across cases, potentially owing to caregiving circumstances or pre-existing support.\n\nConclusion\n\nThe integrated findings suggest the preliminary feasibility of a program combining practical learning and psychological support for older family caregivers. However, the small sample, absence of a control group, and descriptive analyses preclude causal inference. Future studies should recruit larger samples, incorporate health-literacy and caregiving-self-efficacy measures, and use inferential statistics and longitudinal follow-up. The findings offer preliminary input for long-term care policy and family caregiver support-service design in a longevity society.",
      "keywords": "高齡家庭照顧者、成人教育、終身學習、心理韌性、家庭照顧支持服務",
      "keywords_en": "older family caregivers; adult education; lifelong learning; psychological resilience; family caregiver support services",
