      "title": "大語言模型輔助社會處方文獻分析：證據結構、作用機制與政策意涵",
      "title_en": "Large Language Model-Assisted Literature Analysis of Social Prescribing: Evidence Structure, Mechanisms, and Policy Implications",
      "abstract": "社會處方透過醫療、社福或社區端辨識個人非醫療需求，並由連結工作者、導航者或相關專業人員協助服務使用者連結至適切社區資源。近年相關研究快速增加，但其定義、服務模式、介入內容、研究設計與評估指標高度異質。本研究旨在建立結構化社會處方文獻資料庫，運用大語言模型輔助資料萃取與初步分類，分析2015至2025年間英文文獻之證據結構、主要研究主題、作用機制、執行條件及政策意涵。研究檢索PubMed、EMBASE、PsycINFO及Cochrane Library，依預先設定之納入與排除原則進行文獻辨識與篩選，並參考PRISMA原則整理篩選程序，最終納入217篇文獻。大語言模型依固定欄位協助初步擷取研究設計、研究對象與樣本、服務場域、社會處方模式、轉介來源、link worker或導航者、介入內容、追蹤期間、評估指標、主要結果及研究限制，再由研究者進行人工覆核與主題歸納。分析架構包括社會處方模式、介入類型、成效結果、執行條件及研究限制五大面向，共45項代碼；另依文獻主要證據功能區分為較強證據、中等／較直接證據、機制／執行證據、理論／政策／間接證據及研究計畫書／無結果文獻。結果顯示，較強證據7篇（3.2%）、中等／較直接證據50篇（23.0%）、機制／執行證據91篇（41.9%）、理論／政策／間接證據50篇（23.0%），另有19篇（8.8%）為研究計畫書或尚無結果文獻。直接證據70篇（32.3%），部分直接證據33篇（15.2%），合計103篇（47.5%）。主題分析以志願、社區與社會企業部門（VCSE）／社區承接150篇（69.1%）及link worker／轉介138篇（63.6%）最為突出。整體文獻在心理福祉、生活品質、孤獨與社會孤立、社會連結、自評健康及參與者經驗等面向呈現較一致的正向訊號，但具較強因果推論能力之研究仍有限，醫療服務使用及成本效益結果亦未完全一致。機制與執行研究較一致指出，個人化需求評估、可信任的連結工作者、共同決策、適切資源媒合、首次參與支持、持續追蹤、社會歸屬與社區承接能力，是影響社會處方運作的重要條件。社會處方宜被理解為包含需求辨識、個人化評估、共同決策、資源媒合、參與支持、持續追蹤及資訊回饋的完整服務路徑，而非單純活動轉介。大語言模型在本研究中定位為初步萃取、結構化整理與分類之輔助工具；由於尚未設置純人工分析對照、人工修正比例或人機一致率比較，故不據此推論其能提升分析效率或準確性。",
      "abstract_en": "Social prescribing connects individuals with non-medical community resources through referrals from healthcare, social care, or community settings, often supported by link workers, navigators, or related professionals. As the literature has expanded, substantial heterogeneity has emerged in definitions, service models, intervention components, study designs, and outcome measures. This study aimed to construct a structured social prescribing literature database and use a large language model (LLM) to assist preliminary data extraction and classification, with a focus on evidence structure, major research themes, mechanisms, implementation conditions, and policy implications in English-language studies published between 2015 and 2025. PubMed, EMBASE, PsycINFO, and the Cochrane Library were searched. Studies were identified and screened according to predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria, with the screening process organised with reference to PRISMA principles. A total of 217 publications were included. The LLM assisted extraction of study design, population and sample, setting, social prescribing model, referral pathway, link worker or navigator role, intervention, follow-up, outcome measures, key findings, and study limitations, followed by researcher review and thematic synthesis. A 45-code framework covering five domains was developed: social prescribing models, intervention types, outcomes, implementation conditions, and research limitations. Publications were also grouped by primary evidence function. Of the 217 publications, 7 (3.2%) were classified as relatively stronger evidence, 50 (23.0%) as moderate or more direct evidence, 91 (41.9%) as mechanism or implementation evidence, 50 (23.0%) as theoretical, policy, or indirect evidence, and 19 (8.8%) as protocols or studies without outcome data. Seventy publications (32.3%) provided direct evidence and 33 (15.2%) partially direct evidence, for a combined 103 publications (47.5%). The most prominent themes were voluntary, community and social enterprise (VCSE) capacity/community provision (150; 69.1%) and link workers/referral pathways (138; 63.6%). Across the literature, relatively consistent positive signals were observed for psychological well-being, quality of life, loneliness and social isolation, social connectedness, self-rated health, and participant experience. However, studies capable of stronger causal inference remained limited, and findings on healthcare utilisation and cost-effectiveness were inconsistent. Recurrent mechanisms included personalised needs assessment, trusted link-worker relationships, shared decision-making, appropriate resource matching, support for initial participation, continued follow-up, social belonging, and community-sector capacity. Social prescribing should therefore be understood as a service pathway encompassing needs identification, personalised assessment, shared decision-making, resource matching, participation support, follow-up, and feedback rather than as simple referral to an activity. In this study, the LLM was used as an auxiliary tool for preliminary extraction, structuring, and classification. Because no fully manual comparator, correction-rate analysis, or human-machine agreement assessment was conducted, the study does not claim that the LLM improved analytical efficiency or accuracy.",
      "keywords": "社會處方；大語言模型；連結工作者；文獻分析；作用機制；成效評估；健康社會決定因素",
      "keywords_en": "social prescribing; large language model; link worker; literature analysis; mechanisms; outcome evaluation; social determinants of health",
      "display_presenters": "劉宜君、陳怡蒨",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "劉宜君 教授兼學務長｜元智大學社會暨政策科學學系",
        "陳怡蒨 助理教授兼校務研究中心主任｜元智大學社會暨政策科學學系"
      ],
