      "title": "生成式AI介入高齡長者求助路徑之機會、風險與治理課題",
      "title_en": "Generative AI in Older Adults' Help-Seeking Pathways: Opportunities, Risks, and Governance Challenges",
      "abstract": "近年來生成式人工智慧正以不同於過去數位科技的速度與方式進入高齡長者的日常生活，並可能成為新型求助入口。其以自然語言對話即時提供陪伴、解釋與建議，降低操作、表達與資訊理解門檻。以美國為例，50歲以上成年人使用生成式AI的比例自2023至2025年兩年間增加21個百分點；相近增幅在高齡族群的網際網路與智慧型手機普及歷程中，分別約需7年及3年。2025年浙江開放大學與阿里研究院研究亦顯示，高齡者整體AI使用率雖較低，但已使用者呈現較高使用頻率與黏性。生成式AI因而可能成為長者的初步求助對象，本文據此以「求助路徑」為核心，分析生成式AI介入問題辨識、資訊取得、正式專業協助與後續轉介所產生的機會、風險與治理問題。\n\n本文指出，生成式AI介入高齡者求助路徑，同時具有降低求助門檻與放大錯誤信任風險的雙重效果。其可提高資訊可近用性、協助整理問題與理解複雜資訊，並於部分情境提供情緒支持；但便利、流暢且近似專業諮詢的回應，也可能使錯誤或不完整資訊獲得過高信任，進而影響後續判斷與求助。當AI成為健康、財務、法律或生活問題的初步求助對象，原由家屬、社區與專業人員承擔的查核、守門與轉介功能亦可能弱化。高齡者原有之求助行為即受症狀自我評估、獨立性、非正式支持與服務可近性等因素影響，生成式AI若介入問題辨識與資訊取得等前端節點，其回應即可能影響長者是否進入後續正式協助。\n\n對此，現行人工智慧治理雖已逐步重視透明、人為監督與風險管理，卻尚未完整回應一般生成式AI介入求助路徑後的真人接手問題。我國《人工智慧基本法》雖納入人類監督與高風險警示，並特別考量兒少最佳利益，卻未形成相應的高齡者保護與個案接手機制，相關義務仍有待目的事業主管機關以部門法規填補；歐盟《人工智慧法》則對特定高風險用途要求人為監督，但一般消費型生成式AI即使承接重要醫療、財務或法律求助，也未必因此進入高風險規範。現行制度較能回答「使用者是否知道正在與AI互動」，卻較少回答「AI不適合繼續處理時，如何確保真人及時接手」。\n\n本文因此主張，應將「守門與轉介」納入高齡者生成式AI治理的核心機制。對可能造成生命身體、醫療用藥、重大財產、重要權益或緊急服務風險的求助情境，應建立風險辨識、限制揭示、轉介啟動及可實際到達的真人接手管道，使生成式AI成為連結適當協助的入口，而非阻斷後續真人求助的終點。",
      "abstract_en": "Generative AI is entering older adults' everyday lives at a pace unlike earlier digital technologies and may become a new entry point for help-seeking. Through natural-language dialogue it lowers barriers to use, expression, and comprehension. In the United States, the share of adults aged 50 and over using generative AI rose by 21 percentage points between 2023 and 2025; comparable gains took roughly seven years for internet use and three years for smartphones. A 2025 study by Zhejiang Open University and the AliResearch Institute reports that although overall use among older adults is lower, users show higher frequency and more sustained engagement. This paper therefore examines the opportunities, risks, and governance questions arising when generative AI enters problem recognition, information seeking, formal professional assistance, and referral.\n\nGenerative AI both lowers barriers to seeking help and amplifies the risk of misplaced trust. It can improve access to information, help organize problems, and provide some emotional support; yet its fluent, seemingly professional responses may lend excessive credibility to erroneous information and affect subsequent judgment and help-seeking. As it becomes a first point of contact for health, financial, legal, or everyday problems, the checking, gatekeeping, and referral functions carried by family, community, and professionals may weaken. Older adults' help-seeking is already shaped by symptom self-appraisal, independence, informal support, and service accessibility, so responses at these early nodes may bear on whether they reach formal assistance.\n\nCurrent AI governance addresses transparency, human oversight, and risk management, but not how a human takes over once AI enters the pathway. Taiwan's Artificial Intelligence Basic Act incorporates human oversight and high-risk warnings and expressly considers the best interests of children, yet offers no counterpart for older adults, leaving such duties to sectoral regulation. The EU AI Act imposes human oversight on certain high-risk uses, yet consumer generative AI handling significant medical, financial, or legal requests does not necessarily fall within them. Existing rules answer whether users know they are interacting with AI, but not how a human can be reached in time.\n\nThis paper argues that human gatekeeping and referral should be a core governance mechanism here. Where a situation may involve life or physical safety, medication, major financial interests, significant rights, or emergency services, mechanisms should provide for risk identification, disclosure of limitations, activation of referral, and a reachable handover to a person or institution with professional authority and responsibility, so that generative AI becomes an entry point to help rather than an endpoint.",
      "keywords": "生成式人工智慧；高齡者；求助行為；人為監督；人工智慧治理",
      "keywords_en": "Generative AI; older adults; help-seeking; human oversight; AI governance",
