      "title": "尊嚴與資格之間：身障資格、長照評估與被照顧者權益",
      "title_en": "Between Dignity and Eligibility: Disability Certification, Long-Term Care Assessment, and the Rights of Care Recipients",
      "abstract": "隨著臺灣高齡化與長期照顧制度的發展，失能評估已成為取得長照服務的重要門檻。然而，現行福利體系中，身心障礙鑑定與長照失能評估分屬不同制度，其資格認定標準與評估邏輯並不相同。實務上，部分已持有永久性重度身心障礙證明者，在申請長照服務時仍需再次接受失能評估，甚至重新證明自身功能受限與照顧需求。此種重複評估現象不僅涉及制度銜接問題，也可能影響被照顧者的尊嚴、權益與服務可近性。\n\n本文旨在探討身障資格與長照資格之間的制度落差，以及被照顧者在資格認定過程中的經驗與處境。研究採反思性個案研究取徑，以研究者陪同持有永久性重度身心障礙證明之家人接受長照評估的經驗作為關鍵案例，並結合身心障礙鑑定制度、長照失能評估機制及相關政策文件分析，同時參酌共同作者作為一名醫務社工其於身障評估實務中的觀察與經驗進行討論。\n\n研究發現，長照評估不僅是照顧需求的確認程序，更是一種福利資格的認定與生產機制。即使個案已透過身心障礙鑑定取得永久性重度障礙資格，當其進入長照制度後，原有的障礙身分並未必然轉化為照顧資格，而必須重新透過功能評估證明其失能程度與照顧需求。換言之，同一個身體在不同福利制度中可能受到不同分類邏輯的辨識與判定，並在制度轉換之間反覆成為需要被驗證的對象。當資格證明進一步涉及身體檢視或功能展示時，被照顧者亦可能面臨維護身體自主與尊嚴、以及取得照顧資源之間的張力。\n\n本文認為，身障資格與長照資格之間的落差，不宜僅被理解為行政制度銜接不足的技術性問題，而應進一步考察不同福利制度如何透過各自的分類、評估與專業判斷，界定何種身體狀態得以被承認為具有照顧需求。當已獲國家制度認定的障礙者仍須在另一福利體系中反覆證明自身失能時，福利資格的取得便同時涉及行政分類、專業權威與身體經驗。本文藉由分析此一「反覆證明失能」的過程，重新思考福利資格認定如何形塑被照顧者的權利位置，以及尊嚴照顧理念在福利行政實作中的可能與限制。",
      "abstract_en": "As Taiwan’s population continues to age and its long-term care (LTC) system expands, disability assessment has become a crucial gateway to accessing LTC services. However, within the current welfare system, disability certification and LTC eligibility assessment are governed by different institutional frameworks and guided by distinct assessment criteria. In practice, individuals who already hold permanent severe disability certification are often required to undergo additional LTC assessments and repeatedly demonstrate their functional limitations and care needs when applying for LTC services. Such repeated assessments not only reveal a lack of coordination between welfare systems but may also affect care recipients’ dignity, rights, and access to care.\n\nThe findings suggest that LTC assessment is not merely a procedure for identifying care needs but also a mechanism through which welfare eligibility is determined and produced. Even when an individual has already been officially recognized as having a permanent severe disability through the disability certification system, this status does not automatically translate into LTC eligibility. Instead, upon entering the LTC system, the individual must once again demonstrate the extent of their functional limitations and care needs through assessment. In other words, the same body may be recognized and classified according to different institutional logics across welfare systems and may repeatedly become an object of verification as it moves between institutional settings. When such verification further involves bodily examination or the demonstration of bodily functions, care recipients may face tensions between maintaining bodily autonomy and dignity and securing access to necessary care resources.\n\nThis study argues that the gap between disability status and LTC eligibility should not be understood merely as a technical problem of insufficient administrative coordination. Rather, it calls for closer examination of how different welfare systems employ their own classifications, assessments, and forms of professional judgment to determine which bodily conditions are recognized as constituting legitimate care needs. When individuals whose disabilities have already been officially recognized by the state are nevertheless required to repeatedly prove their disability within another welfare system, the acquisition of welfare eligibility becomes intertwined with administrative classification, professional authority, and embodied experience. By examining this process of “repeatedly proving disability,” this study reconsiders how welfare eligibility mechanisms shape the rights and positions of care recipients, as well as the possibilities and limitations of realizing dignity in care within welfare administration.",
      "keywords": "長期照顧、福利資格、制度設計",
      "keywords_en": "Long-Term Care, Welfare Eligibility, Institutional Design",
