      "title": "以幼兒園老幼共學構築跨世代共好",
      "title_en": "Constructing Intergenerational Mutual Well-being through Kindergarten-Based Intergenerational Learning",
      "abstract": "面對高齡化與少子化並行的社會變遷，如何在日常生活與教育場域中創造不同世代之間有意義、可持續且具互惠性的互動機會，已成為高齡福祉、幼兒教育與社區創新的重要議題。既有跨世代研究多著重方案介入後的成效與參與者改變，較少從幼兒園作為持續性教育與社區連結場域的角度，進一步探討老幼共學如何被規劃、推動、調整並逐步形成可延續的實務模式。因此，本研究以幼兒園推動老幼共學之實務經驗為基礎，採質性取向之實務研究，結合行動研究與模式建構觀點，探討跨世代共學在真實教育情境中的推動歷程、互動樣貌、實踐困難與支持需求。\n\n本研究透過活動紀錄、文字資料、影音紀錄與問卷回饋等多元資料來源，整理並分析老幼共學從活動規劃、互動引導、角色參與到經驗擴展的實踐過程，並關注幼兒、高齡者、教師、家長及社區資源在其中所扮演的角色及彼此之間的連結。研究發現，老幼共學並非單次活動或單向陪伴，而是一個需要持續設計、引導與調整的互動歷程。活動內容本身固然重要，但能否形成穩定而有意義的跨世代互動，也與參與角色的投入、教師的引導、家庭的支持，以及社區資源的連結密切相關。透過反覆的共同參與與互動，幼兒與高齡者得以在共享經驗中建立熟悉感與情感連結，教師、家長與社區亦共同構成支持老幼共學持續運作的重要條件。\n\n從實務運作觀點來看，老幼共學的持續性並非僅由單一活動決定，而是建立在多項實踐要素的連續累積之上。活動設計提供跨世代共同參與的基礎，互動引導促進參與關係的形成，角色參與使幼兒、高齡者、教師、家長與社區資源得以在同一實踐歷程中建立連結，而經驗擴展則使既有的互動與關係能延伸至後續活動。這些要素彼此並非相互獨立，而是在反覆實踐與調整的過程中相互影響，共同支持老幼共學由單次活動逐步發展為具有延續性的跨世代實踐。\n\n綜合實務歷程與資料分析結果，本研究進一步提出以幼兒園為平台之跨世代共好實務模式，將活動設計、互動引導、角色參與、經驗擴展與支持資源視為相互連動的實踐要素。此模式不僅呈現老幼共學從單次活動走向持續性實踐的可能路徑，也凸顯幼兒園作為連結兒童、高齡者、家庭與社區之跨世代平台的潛力。相較於將跨世代互動視為個別方案，本研究更強調幼兒園作為長期教育場域所具有的連結與累積作用，並藉由多方角色持續參與，使跨世代互動逐步融入教育與社區實踐。因此，本研究所提出之模式可作為未來教育場域規劃跨世代學習、社區組織推動世代交流，以及高齡服務創新之實務參考。",
      "abstract_en": "Population ageing and declining birth rates are reshaping the social and educational environments in which different generations live and interact. Creating meaningful, sustainable, and mutually beneficial opportunities for intergenerational interaction has therefore become an important issue in ageing welfare, early childhood education, and community innovation. Existing studies on intergenerational programs have often emphasized intervention outcomes and changes among participants, while less attention has been paid to how intergenerational practices are planned, implemented, adjusted, and sustained within kindergarten settings. As kindergartens are not only educational spaces for young children but also potential platforms for connecting families and community resources, examining the practical process of kindergarten-based intergenerational learning may provide useful insights into the development of more sustainable forms of intergenerational engagement.\n\nBased on practical experience in promoting intergenerational learning in a kindergarten, this study adopts a qualitative, practice-oriented approach and integrates perspectives of action research and model building. Multiple sources of data, including activity records, textual materials, video documentation, and questionnaire feedback, were used to examine the implementation process, interaction patterns, practical challenges, and support needs of intergenerational learning. The analysis focused on how activities were designed and guided, how different participants became involved, and how shared experiences were gradually extended through repeated practice. Particular attention was also paid to the roles of children, older adults, teachers, parents, and community resources, as well as to the relationships formed among these participants.\n\nThe findings suggest that intergenerational learning should not be understood as a one-time event or a form of one-way companionship. Rather, it is a gradual and continuously adjusted process shaped by activity design, interaction guidance, role participation, experience expansion, and supporting resources. Meaningful intergenerational interaction emerges not only from the content of individual activities, but also from sustained participation and the connections established among children, older adults, educators, families, and the wider community. Through repeated shared experiences, participants can develop familiarity, emotional connection, and mutual support, while teachers, parents, and community resources provide important conditions for sustaining the practice.\n\nBuilding on these findings, this study proposes a kindergarten-based practical model of intergenerational mutual well-being. The model conceptualizes activity design, interaction guidance, role participation, experience expansion, and supporting resources as interconnected elements of practice. It illustrates a possible pathway for moving from isolated intergenerational activities toward sustained intergenerational learning and highlights the potential of kindergartens to serve as platforms linking children, older adults, families, and communities. The proposed model may provide practical implications for educational settings, community organizations, and innovations in ageing services that seek to promote meaningful and sustainable intergenerational collaboration.",
      "keywords": "老幼共學、跨世代共好、高齡社會、幼兒園、實務模式建構",
      "keywords_en": "Intergenerational Learning; Intergenerational Mutual Well-being; Ageing Society; Kindergarten; Practical Model Building.",
      "display_presenters": "葉桂娥",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "葉桂娥／新竹縣私立佳佳幼兒園／負責人"
      ],
      "author_info_lines_en": []
