      "title": "應用需求幅度理論探討台灣高齡者之生活負擔與樂齡學習增能之研究",
      "title_en": "Life Loads and Learning Empowerment Among Older Adults in Taiwan: An Application of McClusky's Margin Theory of Needs",
      "abstract": "推行樂齡學習是世界各國因應預防老化的策略之一，本研究聚焦在教育部所推出的樂齡學習政策，期望瞭解高齡者在參與樂齡學習後，其生活負擔有無因為學習增能後而有所減輕或改變。本研究以McClusky需求幅度理論（margin theory of needs）為基礎，調查臺灣高齡者在應付需求、表現需求、貢獻需求及影響需求等層面有哪些負擔與需求；並且在參與樂齡學習後，有何增能以及增能程度；最後根據研究結果，提供樂齡學習中心未來課程與教學的改革方向。本研究採單次回溯調查學習前後的差異，並透過發放線上問卷，邀請全國各地區樂齡學習中心學員參與填答，共回收有效樣本800份。\n\n本研究以Cronbach’s α值及因素負荷量檢測信效度，以多元迴歸進行學習增能程度分析。研究結果顯示，高齡者參與學習前對表現需求有較高的負擔，其次依序為影響需求、貢獻需求，最後為應付需求；對應課程需求較高者為體驗活動的學習、老年相關法律知識、居家安全、投資理財、對週遭親友的影響力。參與樂齡學習後，則以表現需求增能程度最高，其次依序為貢獻需求、影響需求，最後為應付需求；對應課程學習增能程度較高者為體驗活動的學習、健康促進、志工服務、社會貢獻。\n\n研究結論表明，樂齡學習有助於高齡者提升生活能量、降低生活負擔，並促進社會參與。但仍有改善空間，建議未來課程應優先強化有高度需求但未被滿足的課程，包含老年相關法律知識、投資理財、參與公共事務等，並採分級化、數位化及體驗式教學設計，以提升高齡教育之學習成效與社會效益。",
      "abstract_en": "Promoting senior learning is one of the strategies adopted by countries worldwide to address population aging. This study focuses on the senior learning policy implemented by the Ministry of Education Republic of China (Taiwan), aiming to examine whether older adults' life burdens have been alleviated or altered as a result of empowerment gained through participation in senior learning programs. Grounded in McClusky's Margin Theory of Needs, this study investigates the burdens and needs experienced by older adults in Taiwan across four dimensions—coping needs, expressive needs, contributive needs, and influence needs—as well as the extent of empowerment gained after participating in senior learning. Based on the findings, the study offers recommendations for future curriculum and instructional reform at senior learning centers. A single retrospective survey design was employed to assess differences before and after learning, using an online questionnaire distributed to learners at senior learning centers across Taiwan. A total of 800 valid responses were collected.\n\nReliability and validity were assessed using Cronbach's α and factor loadings, and multiple regression analysis was conducted to examine the degree of learning empowerment. The results indicate that, prior to participation, older adults experienced the greatest burden in expressive needs, followed by influence needs, contributive needs, and coping needs, respectively. The courses corresponding to higher demand included experiential learning activities, legal knowledge related to aging, home safety, investment and financial management, and influence on family and friends. After participating in senior learning programs, the greatest degree of empowerment was observed in expressive needs, followed by contributive needs, influence needs, and coping needs, respectively. The courses corresponding to the highest degree of learning empowerment included experiential learning activities, health promotion, volunteer service, and social contribution.\n\nThe findings suggest that senior learning helps enhance older adults' vitality, reduce life burdens, and promote social participation. It is recommended that future curricula prioritize strengthening courses with high but unmet demand, such as legal knowledge related to aging, investment and financial management, and participation in public affairs, and adopt tiered, digitalized, and experiential instructional designs to enhance the learning outcomes and social benefits of senior learning.",
      "keywords": "需求幅度理論、樂齡學習、負擔、能量 1國立中正大學成人及繼續教育學系博士生",
      "keywords_en": "margin theory of needs, senior learning, load and power 1 Chi-Jou Liu, Doctoral Student, Department of Adult and Continuing Education, National Chung Cheng University",
      "display_presenters": "劉霽瑈",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "劉霽瑈¹、胡夢鯨²",
        "¹國立中正大學成人及繼續教育學系博士生",
        "²國立中正大學成人及繼續教育學系特聘教授"
      ],
      "author_info_lines_en": []
