      "title": "AI賦能的代間數位敘事課程：促進高齡者數位素養與心理社會福祉之探索性研究",
      "title_en": "AI-Empowered Intergenerational Digital Storytelling: An Exploratory Study on Enhancing Digital Literacy and Psychosocial Well-being Among Older Adults",
      "abstract": "面對台灣超高齡社會的數位落差，本研究發展整合生成式人工智慧（GenAI）的代間數位敘事課程，探討其對高齡學習者數位素養及心理社會福祉之影響。\n\n研究採單組前後測設計，招募台北地區兩所大學32位60歲以上高齡學習者，參與為期8週、每週2.5小時之代間數位敘事課程。課程由大學青年志工以一對多方式協助，教導高齡者使用Suno、Gemini及NotebookLM等GenAI工具完成數位故事創作。教學策略採教師示範後學員自主操作模式，志工僅提供口頭引導協助。課程前後施測數位素養、自我效能、社會支持、主觀活力及自主功能等量表，並以配對樣本t檢定及MANOVA分析。\n\n根據分析結果，課程介入後，群內MANOVA檢定達顯著（Hotelling's T² = 19.92, F(6,26) = 2.78, p = .032, η² = .391），顯示介入後整體成效改善。單變量分析顯示數位素養顯著提升（前測M = 49.88, 後測M = 56.88, t = -3.19, p = .003, Cohen's d = 0.56）。進一步以基線自我效能中位數分組進行群間比較，發現低自我效能組在主觀活力（d = 1.23, p = .002）、社會支持（d = 0.85, p = .023）及自我效能（d = 0.94, p = .013）之提升均顯著優於高自我效能組。\n\n整體而言，本研究初步顯示GenAI融入代間數位敘事課程與60歲以上學習者數位素養及部分心理社會指標的正向改變相關。然而，鑑於未設控制組，尚無法排除重複測量、課程參與或青年志工支持等因素的影響。後續研究宜採對照組或隨機分派設計，並使用保留連續變項資訊的分析方法，進一步檢驗課程效果、作用機制及代間互動歷程。",
      "abstract_en": "Against the backdrop of the digital divide in Taiwan’s super-aged society, this study developed an intergenerational digital storytelling program integrating generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) and examined its effects on digital literacy and psychosocial well-being among older learners.\n\nA one-group pretest–posttest design was adopted. Thirty-two learners aged 60 years and above were recruited from two universities in Taipei to participate in an eight-week intergenerational digital storytelling program, delivered in weekly 2.5-hour sessions. University student volunteers provided one-to-many assistance, supporting older participants in using GenAI tools—including Suno, Gemini, and NotebookLM—to create digital stories. The instructional approach combined teacher demonstration with participants’ independent practice, while volunteers offered verbal guidance only. Digital literacy, self-efficacy, social support, subjective vitality, and autonomous functioning were assessed before and after the program. Paired-sample t tests and multivariate analysis of variance (MANOVA) were conducted.\n\nThe within-subjects MANOVA revealed a significant multivariate effect following the intervention (Hotelling’s T2 = 19.92, F(6,26) = 2.78, = .032, = .391), indicating overall changes in the outcome measures. Univariate analysis showed a significant improvement in digital literacy (pretest M = 49.88, posttest M = 56.88, t = -3.19, = .003, Cohen’s d = 0.56). Exploratory comparisons based on a median split of baseline self-efficacy further indicated that the low-self-efficacy group demonstrated significantly greater gains than the high-self-efficacy group in subjective vitality (d = 1.23, p = .002), social support (d = 0.85, p = .023), and self-efficacy (d = 0.94, p = .013).\n\nOverall, the findings provide preliminary evidence that integrating GenAI into intergenerational digital storytelling may be associated with positive changes in digital literacy and selected psychosocial outcomes among learners aged 60 years and above. However, because the study did not include a control group, the observed changes cannot be disentangled from potential effects of repeated measurement, program participation, or support from student volunteers. Future research should employ comparison groups or randomized designs and analytical approaches that retain continuous-variable information to further examine program effects, underlying mechanisms, and intergenerational interaction processes.",
      "keywords": "代間數位敘事、生成式人工智慧、高齡學習、數位素養、全齡能力發展",
      "keywords_en": "Intergenerational Digital Storytelling, Generative Artificial Intelligence, Later-life Learning, Digital Literacy, Lifelong Capability Development",
