      "title": "五天能改變什麼？短期音樂營隊促進成人持續學習的機制──以爵士基因音樂營為例",
      "title_en": "What Can Five Days Change? Mechanisms of a Short-Term Music Camp in Fostering Adults' Sustained Learning: A Case Study of the Jazz Gene Music Camp",
      "abstract": "成年人投入職場後，常因時間與角色責任中斷音樂學習，一放常達十餘年。然而，仍有人願意撥出五天，參與需與陌生人合作演奏、具挑戰的營隊，甚至連續多年重複參加。本研究以自我決定理論為核心，探討此類短期音樂營隊如何支持成人重返音樂學習，並探討其參與歷程中之轉變。.本研究採個案研究法，以辦理十一年的爵士樂營隊為場域，研究者兼具參與觀察者身分，針對一位營隊負責人、六位參與2026年營隊且重複參加二至七次之四十歲以上學員，進行半結構式深度訪談。\n\n研究發現，營隊並非藉由降低難度吸引成人，而是透過適性分組、彈性選擇學習內容、教師分享挫折真實經驗以及密集練習與演出等設計，同時支持自主、勝任與關係三項心理需求，使不同程度學員能在適切挑戰中累積成功經驗，重建音樂能力感。更關鍵的是，重複參與者呈現明顯轉變：從初期專注個人表現與焦慮不安，逐漸轉向主動規劃學習目標、協助新學員、認識不同樂手，甚至將營隊中建立的關係延伸至場域之外，形成跨地域的自發組團與自主學習。此一由能力重建、自主投入至社群參與的深化歷程，顯示短期密集營隊亦能成為支持成人長期學習的重要場域，其價值有別於常態性課程與樂團。",
      "abstract_en": "After entering the workforce, adults often discontinue musical learning due to time constraints and role responsibilities, with this hiatus typically lasting for decades. Yet, some are willing to set aside five days to participate in a challenging camp that requires collaborating and performing with strangers, returning year after year. Drawing on Self-Determination Theory, this study investigates how such short-term music camps support adults re-entering music learning and examines the transformations that occur throughout their participation. This study adopts a case study approach, focusing on a jazz camp that has been running for 11 years. Leveraging the researcher's insider perspective as a participant-observer, semi-structured, in-depth interviews were conducted with the camp director and 6 adult learners aged over 40 who participated in the 2026 camp and had repeatedly attended 2 to 7 times.\n\nFindings reveal that the camp attracts adults not by lowering difficulty, but through intentional structural designs such as adaptive grouping, flexible learning content, instructors sharing authentic struggles, and intensive rehearsals and performances. These designs simultaneously support the three basic psychological needs of autonomy, competence, and relatedness, enabling learners of varying levels to accumulate successful experiences within optimal challenges and to rebuild their sense of musical competence. More crucially, repeat participants demonstrate a marked transformation: shifting from an initial focus on personal performance and anxiety toward proactively setting learning goals, assisting newcomers, networking with different musicians, and ultimately extending camp-forged relationships beyond the site itself. This results in members forming cross-regional, self-organized bands and sustaining autonomous learning. Ultimately, this journey from competence to community shows that short-term intensive camps can sustain long-term learning, offering a value distinct from that of regular courses and established community ensembles.",
      "keywords": "自我決定理論、成人音樂學習、持續參與、非正規學習、爵士樂營隊",
      "keywords_en": "Self-Determination Theory, Adult Music Learning, Sustained Participation, Non-formal Learning, Jazz Camp",
      "display_presenters": "江翊甄",
