      "title": "從照顧中斷到友善再就業：中高齡婦女重返勞動市場之障礙與支持策略",
      "title_en": "From Care-Related Career Interruptions to Age-Friendly Re-employment: Barriers and Support Strategies for Midlife and Older Women",
      "abstract": "面對人口高齡化、少子女化與勞動力短缺，中高齡婦女已成為不可忽視的潛在勞動力。然而，許多婦女因婚育、家務及家庭照顧責任中斷職涯，重返勞動市場時又同時面臨年齡、性別、職涯中斷與持續照顧責任所形成的複合型障礙。此一現象顯示，中高齡婦女再就業並非單純的個人就業能力或工作意願問題，而涉及家庭照顧分工、勞動制度及公共支持體系之交互作用。\n\n本研究旨在分析中高齡婦女重返勞動市場之主要障礙，並探討現行就業與照顧支持制度間可能存在的落差。研究採文獻分析法與政策文件分析法，以二度就業婦女、中高齡再就業、家庭照顧、社會支持網絡、彈性工作安排及年齡友善職場等相關文獻與政策資料為分析素材，建構「個人、家庭、職場、制度」四層分析架構，透過主題式歸納與跨層比較，檢視不同因素如何共同影響中高齡婦女之再就業歷程。\n\n綜合分析呈現三項主要發現：第一，再就業障礙具有累積性與交織性，年齡、技能落差、性別角色期待、職涯中斷與照顧責任彼此強化；第二，問題核心並非婦女缺乏工作意願，而是既有工作制度與照顧安排之間存在不相容性，使其難以同時維持勞動者與家庭照顧者角色；第三，親友網絡雖能提供情緒、資訊與臨時照顧支持，卻具有不穩定與不均等性，而就業服務、職業訓練、托育及長期照顧等正式制度又多分屬不同體系，形成支持落差。\n\n據此，本研究提出「照顧支持、能力更新、友善職場」三層策略：強化就業服務與托育、長期照顧及喘息服務的跨體系連結；使職業訓練更貼近實際職缺、數位能力與職涯銜接需求；並由企業推動無年齡歧視招募、彈性工時、分階段復職與職務再設計。本文主張，政策應由「要求個人適應勞動市場」轉向「調整勞動與照顧制度以支持個人」，並由政府、企業、家庭與社會共同分擔照顧責任，以提升中高齡婦女穩定就業及持續參與勞動市場的可能性。",
      "abstract_en": "Population ageing, declining fertility, and labor shortages have made midlife and older women an increasingly important potential source of labor. Yet many women interrupt their careers because of marriage, childrearing, housework, and family caregiving. When returning to the labor market, they may encounter intersecting disadvantages associated with age, gender, career interruption, and continuing care responsibilities. Re-employment therefore cannot be understood simply as a matter of employability or willingness to work, but must be examined in relation to the gendered division of care, employment institutions, and public support systems.\n\nThis study examines major barriers to re-employment and the gaps between employment and care-support systems. It employs literature review and policy document analysis, drawing on research and policy materials concerning women’s re-employment, employment among middle-aged and older workers, family caregiving, social support networks, flexible work arrangements, and age-friendly workplaces. The materials are organized into four analytical levels—individual, family, workplace, and institution—and examined through thematic categorization and cross-level comparison.\n\nThree findings emerge. First, re-employment barriers are cumulative and intersectional: age, skill gaps, gender-role expectations, career interruption, and caregiving responsibilities reinforce one another. Second, the central issue is not necessarily a lack of willingness to work, but an institutional mismatch between employment arrangements and caregiving demands, making it difficult to sustain the roles of both worker and family caregiver. Third, family and social networks can provide emotional, informational, and temporary care support, but such assistance is unstable and unequally distributed; meanwhile, employment services, vocational training, childcare, and long-term care are often delivered through separate systems, creating a support gap.\n\nAccordingly, this study proposes a three-layer strategy of care support, skills renewal, and age-friendly workplaces. Policy should strengthen coordination among employment services, childcare, long-term care, and respite services; connect training more closely with actual vacancies, digital competencies, and career re-entry needs; and encourage age-neutral recruitment, flexible schedules, phased return-to-work arrangements, and job redesign. The study argues for a shift from requiring individuals to adapt to the labor market toward reshaping employment and care institutions to support workers through shared responsibility among government, employers, families, and society.",
      "keywords": "中高齡婦女、再就業、照顧責任、照顧支持、年齡友善職場",
      "keywords_en": "midlife and older women; re-employment; caregiving responsibilities; care support; age-friendly workplace",
      "display_presenters": "黃冠豪",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "黃冠豪 碩士生｜國立臺北大學公共行政暨政策學系"
      ],
      "author_info_lines_en": []
